# Boulevard — Modeling Brief

> **Audience:** you, hand-modeling in Blender. I will not auto-generate this car — the game needs real RC character and that comes from you.

## Silhouette and fantasy

An original wedge-coupe RC toy. Short wheelbase, low slung body, aggressive front-end rake, scooped rear, visible antenna post at rear-left. Must read as a "1:18-scale hobby RC" — chunky toy forms over realistic car detailing.

Clean-room: do not model to any specific shipped RC car. Create an original silhouette.

## Dimensions (metres, RC scale)

- Length: 1.0
- Width: 0.6
- Height (roof): 0.44
- Wheelbase: 0.65
- Track width: 0.48
- Wheel radius: 0.12
- Ground clearance: 0.04

Pivot at body centre, feet of wheels sit on y = 0.

## Polycount budget

- Chassis + cabin: ~1200 tri
- Wheels (x4, shared mesh): ~300 tri each
- Antenna: ~60 tri
- Total: ≤ 2500 tri

## Material slots (name them exactly)

- `paint`   — main body colour. Data-driven tint from `cars/boulevard.json` `cosmetic.paintSlots[0]`. Runtime will scan materials whose name contains "paint" (case-insensitive) and swap the base colour.
- `accent`  — secondary colour on cabin / scoop. Data-driven tint from `cosmetic.paintSlots[1]`.
- `rubber`  — tyre. Flat black-grey, roughness ~0.8.
- `chrome`  — wheel hub + bumper detail. Metallic 1.0, roughness 0.2.
- `plastic` — antenna + interior trim.

## UV layout

- Smart-project body, then adjust seams on centre-line for symmetric paint.
- Wheels: single shared UV chart, mirror-instanced (one wheel mesh, 4 transforms).
- Keep texel density consistent across body panels so any future paint decals scale nicely.

## Node naming (required for runtime)

The runtime scans node names for specific strings. Name them exactly:

- `chassis`  — main body node
- `wheel_fl` — front-left wheel
- `wheel_fr` — front-right wheel
- `wheel_rl` — rear-left wheel
- `wheel_rr` — rear-right wheel
- `antenna`  — antenna rod

Wheels should be their own nodes (not parented inside the chassis mesh) so the runtime can spin them around local X based on speed.

## Export

- Format: GLB (glTF 2.0 binary)
- Y-up
- Apply transforms before export
- Save to `public/content/meshes/cars/boulevard.glb`
- Do NOT commit the .glb (path is gitignored). The runtime loads it from the built `dist/` or `public/` directory.

## Reference vocabulary (not for tracing)

Look at real 1:18 RC buggies and wedge-coupe kit cars for proportion and stance. Modern toy-car racers for the cabin bubble and rear-scoop language. Do NOT trace or copy any specific product design.

## Acceptance

1. Loads in Three.js via `GLTFLoader` (the runtime path is wired up in `src/engine/assets.ts` and `src/render/car-mesh.ts`).
2. Paint colour swaps correctly when `cars/boulevard.json` `paintSlots[0]` is changed from `#e0c874` to, say, `#3a8cd4`.
3. At the default chase-camera FOV 68° and the car occupying ~10% of screen height, the car reads as a toy RC rather than a full-scale car.
4. All four wheels are separate nodes and the runtime can rotate them without dragging the chassis along.
5. Bundle impact: ≤ 200 KB compressed (measured on the built `.glb`).

## Workflow tips

- Start in Blender 5.x, Units > Metric > Metre.
- Build from the wheelbase outward — wheels first, chassis next, cabin last, antenna last.
- Use modifiers (Mirror for symmetry, Bevel for edge softening). Apply before export.
- For the paint material, put a "paint" substring in the Blender material name (e.g. `Body_paint`, `AccentPaint`).
- Before exporting, run File → External → Make All Paths Relative.
- Export dialog: Format GLB, Selection Only off, Transform Y-up, Geometry Apply Modifiers on, Materials Export, Animation off.

## Out-of-scope for this brief

- LOD meshes (Phase 2 will author a low-poly variant if needed).
- Damage / crash state.
- Interior detailing beyond a seat bulge.
- Decals and sponsor marks.

## When this brief is fulfilled

Drop the `.glb` into `public/content/meshes/cars/boulevard.glb` and the game will pick it up automatically. The procedural box-car in `src/render/car-mesh.ts` stays as the fallback so development can continue without the GLB present.
